"An open-source spec for Codex orchestration: Symphony. | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/open-source-codex-orchestration-symphony/)\nciteturn60view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn16search2\",\"lineno\":172}); Total lines: 4768\nL68: Not every task fits the Symphony style of work. Some problems still require engineers working directly with interactive Codex sessions, especially ambiguous problems or work that requires strong judgment and expertise. In practice, these are usually the most interesting and enjoyable tasks for our engineers to spend time on.\nL69: The difference is that Symphony can handle the bulk of routine implementation work. That lets engineers focus on a single hard problem at a time instead of constantly context-switching between smaller tasks.\nL70: We also learned that treating agents as rigid nodes in a state machine doesn’t work well. Models get smarter and can solve bigger problems than the box we try to fit them in. Our early versions of agentic work was only asking Codex to implement the task. That approach proved too limiting. Codex is perfectly capable of creating multiple PRs as well as reading review feedback and addressing it.\nL71: So we gave it tools—`gh` CLI, skills to read CI logs, etc.—and now we can ask Codex to do more, like closing old PRs or pulling reports on completed vs. abandoned work. These types of tasks fell way outside the initial feature implementation box.\nL72: So we eventually moved toward giving agents objectives instead of strict transitions, much like a good manager would assign a goal to a direct report on their team. The power of models comes from their ability to reason, so give them tools and context and let them cook.\nL73: ### Using Symphony to build Symphony\nL74: \nL75: When you open the 【5†Symphony repository,⁠(opens in a new window)†github.com】 the first thing you’ll notice is that Symphony is technically just a `SPEC.md` file—a definition of the problem and the intended solution. Rather than building a complex supervision system, we defined the problem and intended solutions, giving agents high-level steering.\nL76: #### Markdown\nL77: `\nL78: \nL79:     1\nL80: \nL81:     # Symphony Service Specification\nL82: \nL83:     2\nL84: \nL85:     3\nL86: \nL87:     Status: Draft v1 (language-agnostic)\nL88: \nL89:     4\nL90: \nL91:     5\nL92: \nL93:     Purpose: Define a service that orchestrates coding agents to get project work done.\nL94: \nL95:     6\nL96: \nL97:     7\nL98: \nL99:     ## 1. Problem Statement\nL100: \nL101:     8\nL102: \nL103:     9\nL104: \nL105:     Symphony is a long-running automation service that continuously reads work from an issue tracker\nL106: \nL107:     10\nL108:     (Linear in this specification version), creates an isolated workspace for each issue, and runs a\nL109: \nL110:     11\nL111: \nL112:     coding agent session for that issue inside the workspace.\nL113: \nL114:     12\nL115: \nL116:     13\nL117: \nL118:     The service solves four operational problems:\nL119: \nL120:     14\nL121: \nL122:     15\nL123: \nL124:     - It turns issue execution into a repeatable daemon workflow instead of manual scripts.\nL125: \nL126:     16\nL127: \nL128:     - It isolates agent execution in per-issue workspaces so agent commands run only inside per-issue\nL129: \nL130:     17\nL131: \nL132:       workspace directories.\nL133: \nL134:     18\nL135: \nL136:     - It keeps the workflow policy in-repo (`WORKFLOW.md`) so teams version the agent prompt and runtime\nL137: \nL138:     19\nL139: \nL140:       settings with their code.\nL141: \nL142:     20\nL143: \nL144:     - It provides enough observability to operate and debug multiple concurrent agent runs.\nL145: \nL146:     21\nL147: \nL148:     22\nL149: \nL150:     Implementations are expected to document their trust and safety posture explicitly. This\nL151: \nL152:     23\nL153:     specification does not require a single approval, sandbox, or operator-confirmation policy; some\nL154: \nL155:     24\nL156: \nL157:     implementations may target trusted environments with a high-trust configuration, while others may\nL158: \nL159:     25\nL160: \nL161:     require stricter approvals or sandboxing.\nL162: \nL163:     26\nL164: \nL165:     27\nL166: \nL167:     Important boundary:\nL168: \nL169:     28\nL170: \nL171:     29\nL172: \nL173:     - Symphony is a scheduler/runner and tracker reader.\nL174: \nL175:     30\nL176:     - Ticket writes (state transitions, comments, PR links) are typically performed by the coding agent\nL177: \nL178:     31\nL179: \nL180:       using tools available in the workflow/runtime environment.\nL181: \nL182:     32\nL183: \nL184:     - A successful run may end at a workflow-defined handoff state (for example `Human Review`), not\nL185: \nL186:     33\nL187: \nL188:       necessarily `Done`.\nL189: \nL190:     34\nL191: \nL192:     35\nL193: \nL194:     ## 2. Goals and Non-Goals\nL195: \nL196:     36\nL197: \nL198:     37\nL199: \nL200:     ### 2.1 Goals\nL201: \nL202:     38\nL203: \nL204:     39\nL205:     - Poll the issue tracker on a fixed cadence and dispatch work with bounded concurrency.\nL206: \nL207:     40\nL208: \nL209:     - Maintain a single authoritative orchestrator state for dispatch, retries, and reconciliation.\nL210: \nL211:     41\nL212: \nL213:     - Create deterministic per-issue workspaces and preserve them across runs.\nL214: \nL215:     42\nL216: \nL217:     - Stop active runs when issue state changes make them ineligible.\nL218: \nL219:     43\nL220: \nL221:     - Recover from transient failures with exponential backoff.\nL222: \nL223:     44\nL224:     - Load runtime behavior from a repository-owned `WORKFLOW.md` contract.\nL225: \nL226:     45\nL227: \nL228:     - Expose operator-visible observability (at minimum structured logs).\nL229: \nL230:     46\nL231: \nL232:     - Support restart recovery without requiring a persistent database.\nL233: \nL234:     47\nL235: \nL236:     48\nL237: \nL238:     ### 2.2 Non-Goals\nL239: \nL240:     49\nL241: \nL242:     50\nL243: \nL244:     - Rich web UI or multi-tenant control plane.\nL245: \nL246:     51\nL247: \nL248:     - Prescribing a specific dashboard or terminal UI implementation.\nL249: \nL250:     52\nL251:     - General-purpose workflow engine or distributed job scheduler.\nL252: \nL253:     53\nL254: \nL255:     - Built-in business logic for how to edit tickets, PRs, or comments. (That logic lives in the\nL256: \nL257:     54\nL258: \nL259:       workflow prompt and agent tooling.)\nL260: \nL261:     55\nL262: \nL263:     - Mandating strong sandbox controls beyond what the coding agent and host OS provide.\nL264: \nL265:     56\nL266: \nL267:     - Mandating a single default approval, sandbox, or operator-confirmation posture for all\nL268: \nL269:     57\nL270: \nL271:       implementations.\nL272: \nL273:     58\nL274: \nL275:     59\nL276: \nL277:     ## 3. System Overview\nL278: \nL279:     60\nL280: \nL281:     61\nL282: \nL283:     ### 3.1 Main Components\nL284: \nL285:     62\nL286: \nL287:     63\nL288: \nL289:     1. `Workflow Loader`\nL290: \nL291:     64\nL292: \nL293:        - Reads `WORKFLOW.md`.\nL294: \nL295:     65\nL296: \nL297:        - Parses YAML front matter and prompt body.\nL298: \nL299:     66\nL300: \nL301:        - Returns `{config, prompt_template}`.\nL302: \nL303:     67\nL304: \nL305:     68\nL306: \nL307:     2. `Config Layer`\nL308: \nL309:     69\nL310: \nL311:        - Exposes typed getters for workflow config values.\nL312: \nL313:     70\nL314:        - Applies defaults and environment variable indirection.\nL315: \nL316:     71\nL317: \nL318:        - Performs validation used by the orchestrator before dispatch.\nL319: \nL320:     72\nL321: \nL322:     73\nL323: \nL324:     3. `Issue Tracker Client`\nL325: \nL326:     74\nL327: \nL328:        - Fetches candidate issues in active states.\nL329: \nL330:     75\nL331: \nL332:        - Fetches current states for specific issue IDs (reconciliation).\nL333: \nL334:     76\nL335: \nL336:        - Fetches terminal-state issues during startup cleanup.\nL337: \nL338:     77\nL339:        - Normalizes tracker payloads into a stable issue model.\nL340: \nL341:     78\nL342: \nL343:     79\nL344: \nL345:     4. `Orchestrator`\nL346: \nL347:     80\nL348: \nL349:        - Owns the poll tick.\nL350: \nL351:     81\nL352: \nL353:        - Owns the in-memory runtime state.\nL354: \nL355:     82\nL356: \nL357:        - Decides which issues to dispatch, retry, stop, or release.\nL358: \nL359:     83\nL360: \nL361:        - Tracks session metrics and retry queue state.\nL362: \nL363:     84\nL364: \nL365:     85\nL366: \nL367:     5. `Workspace Manager`\nL368: \nL369:     86\nL370: \nL371:        - Maps issue identifiers to workspace paths.\nL372:     87\nL373: \nL374:        - Ensures per-issue workspace directories exist.\nL375: \nL376:     88\nL377: \nL378:        - Runs workspace lifecycle hooks.\nL379: \nL380:     89\nL381: \nL382:        - Cleans workspaces for terminal issues.\nL383: \nL384:     90\nL385: \nL386:     91\nL387: \nL388:     6. `Agent Runner`\nL389: \nL390:     92\nL391: \nL392:        - Creates workspace.\nL393: \nL394:     93\nL395: \nL396:        - Builds prompt from issue + workflow template.\nL397: \nL398:     94\nL399: \nL400:        - Launches the coding agent app-server client.\nL401: \nL402:     95\nL403: \nL404:        - Streams agent updates back to the orchestrator.\nL405:     96\nL406: \nL407:     97\nL408: \nL409:     7. `Status Surface` (optional)\nL410: \nL411:     98\nL412: \nL413:        - Presents human-readable runtime status (for example terminal output, dashboard, or other\nL414: \nL415:     99\nL416: \nL417:          operator-facing view).\nL418: \nL419:     100\nL420: \nL421:     101\nL422: \nL423:     8. `Logging`\nL424: \nL425:     102\nL426: \nL427:        - Emits structured runtime logs to one or more configured sinks.\nL428: \nL429:     103\nL430: \nL431:     104\nL432: \nL433:     ### 3.2 Abstraction Levels\nL434: \nL435:     105\nL436: \nL437:     106\nL438:     Symphony is easiest to port when kept in these layers:\nL439: \nL440:     107\nL441: \nL442:     108\nL443: \nL444:     1. `Policy Layer` (repo-defined)\nL445: \nL446:     109\nL447: \nL448:        - `WORKFLOW.md` prompt body.\nL449: \nL450:     110\nL451: \nL452:        - Team-specific rules for ticket handling, validation, and handoff.\nL453: \nL454:     111\nL455: \nL456:     112\nL457: \nL458:     2. `Configuration Layer` (typed getters)\nL459: \nL460:     113\nL461: \nL462:        - Parses front matter into typed runtime settings.\nL463: \nL464:     114\nL465:        - Handles defaults, environment tokens, and path normalization.\nL466: \nL467:     115\nL468: \nL469:     116\nL470: \nL471:     3. `Coordination Layer` (orchestrator)\nL472: \nL473:     117\nL474: \nL475:        - Polling loop, issue eligibility, concurrency, retries, reconciliation.\nL476: \nL477:     118\nL478: \nL479:     119\nL480: \nL481:     4. `Execution Layer` (workspace + agent subprocess)\nL482: \nL483:     120\nL484: \nL485:        - Filesystem lifecycle, workspace preparation, coding-agent protocol.\nL486: \nL487:     121\nL488: \nL489:     122\nL490: \nL491:     5. `Integration Layer` (Linear adapter)\nL492:     123\nL493: \nL494:        - API calls and normalization for tracker data.\nL495: \nL496:     124\nL497: \nL498:     125\nL499: \nL500:     6. `Observability Layer` (logs + optional status surface)\nL501: \nL502:     126\nL503: \nL504:        - Operator visibility into orchestrator and agent behavior.\nL505: \nL506:     127\nL507: \nL508:     128\nL509: \nL510:     ### 3.3 External Dependencies\nL511: \nL512:     129\nL513: \nL514:     130\nL515: \nL516:     - Issue tracker API (Linear for `tracker.kind: linear` in this specification version).\nL517: \nL518:     131\nL519: \nL520:     - Local filesystem for workspaces and logs.\nL521: \nL522:     132\nL523: \nL524:     - Optional workspace population tooling (for example Git CLI, if used).\nL525: \nL526:     133\nL527: \nL528:     - Coding-agent executable that supports JSON-RPC-like app-server mode over stdio.\nL529: \nL530:     134\nL531: \nL532:     - Host environment authentication for the issue tracker and coding agent.\nL533: \nL534:     135\nL535: \nL536:     136\nL537: \nL538:     ## 4. Core Domain Model\nL539: \nL540:     137\nL541: \nL542:     138\nL543: \nL544:     ### 4.1 Entities\nL545: \nL546:     139\nL547: \nL548:     140\nL549: \nL550:     #### 4.1.1 Issue\nL551: \nL552:     141\nL553: \nL554:     142\nL555:     Normalized issue record used by orchestration, prompt rendering, and observability output.\nL556: \nL557:     143\nL558: \nL559:     144\nL560: \nL561:     Fields:\nL562: \nL563:     145\nL564: \nL565:     146\nL566: \nL567:     - `id` (string)\n"
